WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.485
A payment, pay-77, needs one decision.

2
00:00:05.485 --> 00:00:09.809
One program wants it captured: the money moves to the shop.

3
00:00:09.809 --> 00:00:12.374
Another wants it voided: cancelled.

4
00:00:12.374 --> 00:00:13.180
Never both.

5
00:00:13.333 --> 00:00:19.080
Three computers keep the decision, messages get lost, and any program can stop at any moment.

6
00:00:19.080 --> 00:00:26.558
So if the program that started the decision crashes halfway, how does the next one finish it without changing the answer?

7
00:00:26.558 --> 00:00:27.732
Hold that question.

8
00:00:27.933 --> 00:00:33.028
Picture three clerks with notebooks, and two managers giving them instructions.

9
00:00:33.028 --> 00:00:38.317
The managers, called proposers, are the programs: P1 wants capture, P2 wants void.

10
00:00:38.317 --> 00:00:41.735
The clerks are the three computers, called acceptors.

11
00:00:41.933 --> 00:00:49.608
Each notebook has two lines: the request number the clerk has promised to listen to, and the last instruction it wrote down.

12
00:00:49.608 --> 00:00:53.445
The notebook is the computer's disk, so it survives a restart.

13
00:00:53.600 --> 00:01:00.383
Each request carries a number, round dot proposer, so 2.2 is round 2 from P2, and beats 1.1.

14
00:01:00.383 --> 00:01:07.608
A value is chosen once two clerks out of three, a majority, have written it under the same number.

15
00:01:07.867 --> 00:01:09.391
Let's play it slowly.

16
00:01:09.391 --> 00:01:13.382
P1 picks number 1.1 and sends a prepare to every clerk.

17
00:01:13.382 --> 00:01:20.132
It means: promise to ignore anything lower than 1.1, and tell me what you've already written.

18
00:01:20.333 --> 00:01:24.357
A1 and A2 write promised 1.1, and answer with a promise.

19
00:01:24.357 --> 00:01:26.512
Neither has written any value.

20
00:01:26.512 --> 00:01:30.679
Two out of three is a majority, so P1 doesn't wait for A3.

21
00:01:30.833 --> 00:01:34.699
No one reported a value, so P1 may use its own.

22
00:01:34.699 --> 00:01:37.085
It sends accept 1.1, capture.

23
00:01:37.085 --> 00:01:39.059
A1 and A2 write it down.

24
00:01:39.059 --> 00:01:40.951
The copy to A3 is lost.

25
00:01:41.133 --> 00:01:46.059
Two notebooks out of three now say 1.1 capture, so capture is chosen.

26
00:01:46.059 --> 00:01:49.915
And then P1 crashes, before a single reply reaches it.

27
00:01:50.100 --> 00:01:53.937
So P1 had the best moment of its career and never found out.

28
00:01:53.937 --> 00:02:00.204
Nobody knows yet, and that's the point: chosen is a fact about the notebooks, not about who heard.

29
00:02:00.467 --> 00:02:03.413
Now P2 hears nothing, and its timer runs out.

30
00:02:03.413 --> 00:02:06.622
The obvious move is to start fresh and push void.

31
00:02:06.622 --> 00:02:12.647
But capture is already chosen, so some services would move the money while others cancel it.

32
00:02:12.833 --> 00:02:19.848
What we want is for the next manager to find out what may already be decided, before saying anything.

33
00:02:19.848 --> 00:02:21.863
That's the first round's job.

34
00:02:21.863 --> 00:02:25.266
P2 picks a higher number, 2.2, and sends prepare.

35
00:02:25.266 --> 00:02:27.836
A1 is slow, so only A2 and A3 answer.

36
00:02:28.033 --> 00:02:31.356
A3 promises 2.2, with nothing written.

37
00:02:31.356 --> 00:02:37.476
A2 raises its promise to 2.2, and reports what it wrote: 1.1, capture.

38
00:02:37.700 --> 00:02:42.255
So P2 wants void, and one of its two promises carries capture.

39
00:02:42.255 --> 00:02:43.798
What must it propose?

40
00:02:43.798 --> 00:02:44.973
Pause and guess.

41
00:02:48.233 --> 00:02:48.733
Capture.

42
00:02:48.733 --> 00:02:55.172
The rule is: propose the value with the highest number among the promises, or your own if there's none.

43
00:02:55.172 --> 00:02:56.547
It's a shift handover.

44
00:02:56.547 --> 00:03:03.236
The colleague who takes over finishes the job that's already signed, and keeps their own opinion for later.

45
00:03:03.367 --> 00:03:05.665
P2 sends accept 2.2, capture.

46
00:03:05.665 --> 00:03:14.303
A2 and A3 write it down, and P2 learns the decision: capture, the same value that was chosen when P1 crashed.

47
00:03:14.567 --> 00:03:16.028
Was that luck?

48
00:03:16.028 --> 00:03:16.341
No.

49
00:03:16.341 --> 00:03:19.265
P1's majority was A1 and A2.

50
00:03:19.265 --> 00:03:22.188
P2's majority was A2 and A3.

51
00:03:22.400 --> 00:03:26.452
Any two majorities of three share at least one clerk, here A2.

52
00:03:26.452 --> 00:03:34.818
A value that's chosen sits in a majority of notebooks, so the next manager's majority always includes someone who wrote it down.

53
00:03:34.818 --> 00:03:36.713
Once chosen, it stays chosen.

54
00:03:36.967 --> 00:03:41.700
What we just played is Paxos, Leslie Lamport's method for deciding one value.

55
00:03:41.700 --> 00:03:47.540
The request number is called a ballot, and the two rounds are prepare and promise, then accept.

56
00:03:47.733 --> 00:03:52.074
Lamport first told it as a story about a Greek island's parliament.

57
00:03:52.074 --> 00:03:56.350
Readers struggled, so his next paper was called Paxos Made Simple.

58
00:03:56.350 --> 00:03:58.876
A title like that is mostly an apology.

59
00:03:58.876 --> 00:04:02.245
The whole method is the two rounds you just watched.

60
00:04:02.500 --> 00:04:04.617
Now let's break it on purpose.

61
00:04:04.617 --> 00:04:12.028
The lesson's demo plays 500 runs with five acceptors, lost and repeated messages, and crashes everywhere.

62
00:04:12.200 --> 00:04:16.400
Paxos as written: zero runs out of 500 chose two values.

63
00:04:16.400 --> 00:04:22.325
Now let proposers ignore the promises and push their own value: 114 runs split.

64
00:04:22.325 --> 00:04:31.100
Now let acceptors forget their notebook when they restart, as if they answered before writing to disk: 36 runs split.

65
00:04:31.233 --> 00:04:34.718
Well, that's a lot of payments both captured and cancelled.

66
00:04:34.718 --> 00:04:41.039
So every rule matters: read what's written, carry the highest forward, and write to disk before you answer.

67
00:04:41.300 --> 00:04:42.738
So what does it cost?

68
00:04:42.738 --> 00:04:45.135
One decision takes two round trips.

69
00:04:45.135 --> 00:04:50.408
A majority must be up: three acceptors survive one failure, five survive two.

70
00:04:50.567 --> 00:04:55.684
And plain Paxos decides one value, but a real system needs a log of them.

71
00:04:55.684 --> 00:05:03.746
So real systems run Multi-Paxos: a steady leader runs the first round once, then each command costs one round trip.

72
00:05:03.746 --> 00:05:06.971
Google's lock service, Chubby, works this way.

73
00:05:07.200 --> 00:05:13.471
So the moral: before you propose, ask a majority what they've written, and carry the highest forward.

74
00:05:13.471 --> 00:05:19.245
Two majorities always share a notebook, so a crash can delay a chosen value, never change it.

75
00:05:19.533 --> 00:05:20.719
One quick check.

76
00:05:20.719 --> 00:05:24.721
Suppose P2's promises had come from A1 and A3 instead.

77
00:05:24.721 --> 00:05:27.462
Would it still have proposed capture?

78
00:05:27.462 --> 00:05:28.648
Pause and think.

79
00:05:31.900 --> 00:05:32.209
Yes.

80
00:05:32.209 --> 00:05:36.306
A1 wrote 1.1 capture, and A1 is in that majority too.

81
00:05:36.306 --> 00:05:42.800
Any two clerks out of three include A1 or A2, so every majority hears about capture.
